    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Potosí was founded in 1545 beside a mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and grew to a population that eventually exceeded 200,000.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> was won by Indigenous workers, many of them conscripted each year under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, who carried ore up shafts by candlelight, and many died or were seriously injured in the mines and the refining.</li><li>The easily smelted ore was exhausted by 1565, mercury amalgamation took its place, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> overtook the mountain in the 18th century, and from 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> as the main mineral.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a> produced an estimated 60% of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mined in the world during the second half of the 16th century, and a Spanish expression, valer un Potosí, still means to be worth a great deal.</p></aside>
    </section>

