    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1545</span><span class="rf-text">Founded as a mining town after the traditional discovery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1565</span><span class="rf-text">The ore that could be smelted directly is exhausted and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> production falls sharply</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1603</span><span class="rf-text">An anonymous description counts at least 120,000 native Andeans among the city's residents</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1609</span><span class="rf-text">The pan amalgamation process is invented in Potosí</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1622</span><span class="rf-text">Armed conflict between the Basque and Vicuña factions begins and lasts until 1625</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1689</span><span class="rf-text">The number of mitayos has dropped to about 4,000</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1809</span><span class="rf-text">The Bolivian War of Independence begins, and the city passes between Royalist and Patriot forces</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text">Low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> prices bring a change to the mining of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huayna-capac.jpg" alt="Huayna Capac" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hp_inka11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Potosí lies at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro de Potosí</a>, also called the Cerro Rico, the “rich mountain”, which is popularly conceived of as made of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ore. The city stands at a nominal 4,067 m, and the peak of the mountain is 4,824 m above sea level. The ore lies in veins in a dacite volcanic dome that was extruded about 13.8 million years ago, and the hill is honeycombed with underground workings that reach from the summit to depths of 1,150 m. Diego Huallpa, an indigenous prospector, is traditionally credited with the discovery of the Cerro Rico in 1545, and the town was founded as a mining town in that year.</p><p>There is no authoritative etymology for the name. According to legend, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> saw the hill on a journey from his mines at Ccolque Porco and Andaccaua, said that it must have much silver in its heart, and ordered his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to work it. They heard a thunderous noise and a voice that said the silver was destined for other masters, and the word they used for the noise, “Potocsí”, was said to have become the name. Potosí is believed to be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> word, but in Quechua the root p’otoj does not refer to a thunderous noise, whereas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> it does. Several Quechua speakers explain it instead as an onomatopoeic word for the sound of the hammer against the ore.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The town produced great wealth quickly, and its population eventually exceeded 200,000. The miners at first worked rich oxidised ores containing native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and silver chloride, which could be fed directly into smelting furnaces. Especially successful were small clay furnaces called guayras, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> had used. By 1565 this ore was exhausted and production fell sharply. It recovered with the patio process, invented by the Spanish merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-medina">Bartolomé de Medina</a> in 1554, which used mercury amalgamation to extract silver from lower-grade ores, and from 1609 with the pan amalgamation process, invented in Potosí and better adapted to its conditions. At the peak of production in the 16th and 17th centuries the ore contained up to 40% silver.</p><p>The Spanish crown profited most. It let private entrepreneurs work mines under licence and taxed their profits heavily, among other levies by the quinto, a 20% severance tax on gross value. Coins called pieces of eight were struck at the Potosí mint. The silver went by llama and mule train to the Pacific coast, by ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a>, across the isthmus by mule train to Nombre de Dios or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a>, and from there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on the treasure fleets. Some went east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, and some to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, from where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> carried it to buy Asian goods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> surpassed Potosí as a source of silver in the 18th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano.jpg" alt="Manuel Belgrano" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_belgrano_by_carbonier_retouched.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to Casimir Carbonnier, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Potosí lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a>, in the region the Spanish called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>, and it was important enough to be designated a Villa Imperial in the hierarchy of Spanish urban settlements. Its core was laid out in the standard Spanish grid, while the rancherías, the Indigenous settlements on the northern flank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a>, were more haphazard. A Spanish corregidor, two alcaldes and a town council of 19 men governed it. The crown bureaucracy was a significant presence, five public scribes and some forty independent notaries recorded transactions and wills, and an ecclesiastical court heard cases concerning the clergy. The Inquisition had no tribunal in the city and relied on a commissioner.</p><p>In the early 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, who made up a substantial number of the inhabitants, formed a confederation opposed to the Vicuñas, a group of natives and non-Basque Spanish and Portuguese colonists, and the two fought for control of ore extraction and its management. Armed conflict lasted from 1622 to 1625. The crown intervened and at one point sided with the Basques, and the factions settled with a marriage between the children of their leaders.</p><p>During the Bolivian War of Independence (1809 to 1825) the city passed repeatedly between Royalist and Patriot forces. When the Second Auxiliary Army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a> was forced to retreat, Belgrano decided to blow up the Casa de la Moneda, but the natives undid the fuse, since many people refused to leave and would have died. Two more expeditions from Buenos Aires took Potosí.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Indigenous workers were required to work in the mines under the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a> system, based on a traditional Andean mit’a that had directed labour to public works and collective farming. Workers came from an area of almost 200,000 square miles, and thirteen thousand men were conscripted each year, about one in seven adult males of the Indigenous population. Skilled workers extracted the ore, while the mitayos carried it up in baskets, leather bags or cloth sacks. The loads often weighed 100 to 300 lbs, and the men climbed rickety ladders in steep, narrow shafts lit by a candle tied to the forehead. Many died or were seriously injured in falls and accidents. Pneumonia was a constant danger, as men climbed from the heat of the deep shafts to the cold at 16,000 feet, and mercury poisoning killed many in the refining.</p><p>Mine and mill owners notoriously ignored the rules on provisions and withheld travel money, and the cost of travelling to Potosí and back could exceed a mitayo’s pay for a year. Many stayed on as wage workers, and former mitayos, exempt from the draft, usually earned more. A 1603 report counted 58,800 Indians at work, of whom 5,100 were mitayos, 10,500 were mingas, or contract workers, and 43,200 were free wage earners. Noble David Cook takes this to show that mita labour was one form of work among several. Peter Bakewell stresses its role: as few as 4,500 mitayos worked at a time, because the up to 13,500 conscripted each year were divided in three, each part working one week in three, and many other workers were mitayos in their weeks off or former mitayos.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a> changed the region around it. Wives and children moved to Potosí with the workers, and thousands more fled their villages and gave up their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllu</a> land rights to escape the draft. The affected area stretched, in rough terms, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. By the late 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> had lost nearly 50% of its Indigenous population compared with a little over a century earlier. This increased the burden on those who remained, and at some point in the 1600s up to half of the eligible men might find themselves working at Potosí.</p><p>By 1689 the number of mitayos had fallen to about 4,000. The Viceroy, the Duke of Palata, tried to raise it again with a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> that included the forasteros, who were not subject to the mita. It is suspected that Spanish authorities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Chuquisaca</a> and Oruro did not respond to calls from Potosí to find those who had escaped, since many had proved useful on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> of influential Spaniards. The reform failed. The Duke’s successor set the official number at 4,108 mitayos, 1,367 of them active each week, and in practice it was lower still, because more and more men bought their way out of the obligation.</p><p>The ore itself failed too. The direct-smelting ore was exhausted by 1565, and the mountain was later overtaken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>. In 1891 low <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> prices prompted a change to mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, which continued until 1985.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/don-quixote.jpg" alt="Don Quixote" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Title_page_first_edition_Don_Quijote.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan de la Cuesta, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Potosí gave Spanish a lasting expression, valer un Potosí, “to be worth a Potosí”, meaning to be of great value, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> mentions it in the second part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-quixote">Don Quixote</a> as a land of extraordinary richness. One theory holds that the mint mark of Potosí, the letters PTSI superimposed on one another, is the origin of the dollar sign. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was named after it, and so were the lead-mining towns of Potosi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, and Potosi, Missouri, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining town of Potosi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a> is the world’s largest silver deposit, and it had produced up to 60,000 tonnes by 1996. Estimates are that much silver still remains in the mines. Workers in the mines have offered coca leaves and alcohol to statues of el Tío, a figure of the mountain itself, to protect them. A considerable amount of the colonial architecture has been preserved in the historic centre, and the centre and the Cerro Rico together form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Large churches, lavishly decorated inside, were built in the colonial city, and no convent for women existed there until 1650.</p><p>Potosí is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Potosí and its largest urban centre, and it continues to be an important mining centre. The Potosí Silversmithing School was established in 2011 as part of an industrialisation effort.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Potosí, in the Bolivian Andes, was founded in 1545 at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerro-rico">Cerro Rico</a> and became a Spanish imperial city of more than 200,000 people. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> was mined by Indigenous workers, many of them conscripted under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">mita</a>, at a heavy cost in lives. It is now the capital of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</em></p>

